 duration: "8 min",
 teacherNotes: "On screen or on On the whiteboard, write the common confusion below: 'Fossil 1: Treating 1 as a prime. ✗ 1 is NOT a prime number. A prime must have exactly two factors: 1 and itself. 1 has only one factor (itself), so it is neither prime nor composite.' againwrite: 'Fossil 2: Writing a factorisation that still has a composite. ✗ For example, 30 = 2 × 15 is NOT complete prime factorisation, because 15 is still composite (15 = 3 × 5). The correct prime factorisation is 30 = 2 × 3 × 5. All factors must be prime.' give examples: 'Express 24 as a product of its prime factors. ✗ Wrong: 24 = 3 × 8 (8 is composite). ✗ Wrong: 24 = 4 × 6 (both composite). ✓ Correct: 24 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 (all prime).' againgive examples: 'Is 1 a prime number? ✗ No. 1 is neither prime nor composite. Prime numbers start from 2.'",
