 duration: "10 min",
 teacherNotes: "teachermodels onecompleteexample questions (writeoutdividesolvesteps). example questions 1: Find the prime factorisation of 60. steps: ① Start with the smallest prime 2. 60 ÷ 2 = 30. So 60 = 2 × 30. ② 30 is still composite. 30 ÷ 2 = 15. So 60 = 2 × 2 × 15. ③ 15 is still composite. 15 ÷ 3 = 5. So 60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5. ④ All factors are now prime (2, 2, 3, 5). Answer: 60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5. againdoone: Express 72 as a product of its prime factors. steps: ① 72 ÷ 2 = 36. So 72 = 2 × 36. ② 36 ÷ 2 = 18. So 72 = 2 × 2 × 18. ③ 18 ÷ 2 = 9. So 72 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 9. ④ 9 ÷ 3 = 3. So 72 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. ⑤ All factors are prime. Answer: 72 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. againdoonerecognized'tquestions: Which of the following is a prime number: 1, 9, 13, 15? steps: ① 1 is not prime (neither prime nor composite). ② 9 = 3 × 3 (composite). ③ 13 has only factors 1 and 13 (prime). ④ 15 = 3 × 5 (composite). Answer: 13. childatpaperabovecopyonetimesexample questionsteps. ",
