 name: "example questions",
 duration: "10 min",
 teacherNotes: "teacher models three complete examples questions (Write out reasoning steps). example questions 1 (increase a quantity by r%): Increase 80 by 10%. Show your working steps. steps: ① Original = 80. Increase by 10%. ② 10% of 80 = (10 ÷ 100) × 80 = 0.1 × 80 = 8. ③ New value = original + increase = 80 + 8 = 88. Answer: 88. (Or: new = 80 × (1 + 10/100) = 80 × 1.1 = 88.) example questions 2 (decrease a quantity by r%, should use questions word questions with discount): A bag was S$60. The shop gave a 20% discount. What is the discounted price? Show your working steps. steps: ① Original price = S$60. Discount = 20%. ② 20% of S$60 = (20 ÷ 100) × 60 = 0.2 × 60 = 12. ③ Discounted price = original − discount = S$60 − S$12 = S$48. Answer: S$48. (Or: new = S$60 × (1 − 20/100) = S$60 × 0.8 = S$48.) example questions 3 (percentage point change, should use questions word questions with rate): A club membership rate increased from 25% to 40%. (i) By how many percentage points did the rate increase? (ii) Is this a 15% increase of the old rate? Explain clearly. Show your working. steps: (i) ① Old rate = 25%. New rate = 40%. ② Percentage-point increase = new − old = 40% − 25% = 15 percentage points. Answer (i): 15 percentage points. (ii) ① Is this a 15% increase? Check: 15% of 25% = (15 ÷ 100) × 25 = 3.75. ② So a 15% increase of 25% would be 25 + 3.75 = 28.75%, not 40%. ③ Answer (ii): No, it is not a 15% increase. It is a 15 percentage-point increase. A 15% increase of 25% would only reach 28.75%, not 40%. Have the childcomprehensionthreequestionstotalsamepoint: increasesubtracttimefirstcalculate r% of original, againadd or subtract; percentpointisstraightconnectmutualsubtract, notpercentageincreaselong. ",
