 teacherNotes: "summarythis weekfocus: (1) official Sec 1 AEIS math paper format: Part 1 MCQ 34 questions 30 min + Part 2 show working 20+10–15 questions 1 hour 45 min, without calculator. (2) this weekcontent: Sec 1 NUMBER AND ALGEBRA, N3. Percentage: 3.4 increasing/decreasing a quantity by a given percentage (including concept of percentage point) (usepercentageincreasesubtractnumberquantity, includingpercentpointconcept), corresponds to preceding level rule. (3) this week High-frequency error: adding the percentage value instead of the percentage of the quantity (straightconnectaddpercentageandnotaddnumberquantitypercentage: 80 increased by 10% errorwritebecome 90, shouldis 88); treating a percentage-point change as a percentage increase (percentpointchangeswhenpercentageincreaselong: 40% → 50% error Sayis 10% increaselong, shouldis 10 percentpointincreaselong). (4) Increase a quantity by r%: new = original × (1 + r/100) (increase: newvalue = originalvalue × (1 + r/100)). method: firstcalculate r% of original, againadd. example: 80 increased by 10% = 10% of 80 = 8, 80 + 8 = 88 ( or 80 × 1.1 = 88). (5) Decrease a quantity by r%: new = original × (1 − r/100) (decrease: newvalue = originalvalue × (1 − r/100)). example: 80 decreased by 10% = 10% of 80 = 8, 80 − 8 = 72 ( or 80 × 0.9 = 72). (6) Percentage point (percentpoint, officialtechniquelanguage): a change from 40% to 50% is 10 percentage points (from 40% to 50% is 10 percentpoint). It is NOT a 10% increase (not 10% increaselong, because 10% increase of 40% would be 44%). Percentage-point change = new rate − old rate (percentpointchanges = newcomparerate − oldcomparerate, straightconnectmutualsubtract). (7) should use questions word questions with price increase: A price of S$60 is increased by 20%. What is the new price? Solution: 20% of S$60 = 12. New price = S$60 + S$12 = S$72 ( or S$60 × 1.2 = S$72). (8) should use questions word questions with discount: A bag was S$60. The shop gave a 20% discount. What is the discounted price? Solution: 20% of S$60 = 12. Discounted price = S$60 − S$12 = S$48 ( or S$60 × 0.8 = S$48). (9) should use questions word questions with rate change: A club membership rate increased from 25% to 40%. (i) By how many percentage points? Answer: 40% − 25% = 15 percentage points. (ii) Is this a 15% increase of the old rate? Answer: No. A 15% increase of 25% would be 25 + (15% of 25) = 28.75%, not 40%. So it's a 15 percentage-point increase (not a 15% increase). (10) keysteps: Step 1: Identify the original quantity or rate. Step 2: Calculate r% of the original: r% of N = (r ÷ 100) × N. Step 3: For increase, add; for decrease, subtract (increasetimemutualadd, decreasetimemutualsubtract: increase: new = original + (r% of original); decrease: new = original − (r% of original)). Step 4: For percentage point, subtract rates directly (percentpointchanges: straightconnectmutualsubtract new rate − old rate, notpercentageincreaselong). Step 5: State the answer with units and interpret. (11) amountusenewaddslopedollar S$ (money in Singapore dollars S$). (12) onlyoneproperty unique keys: twooptioncannotissameoneamount. notneedsametimeraiseprovide 88 and 80 + 8 as two options (thatissameonevalue). notneedatsameone questionmiddlesametimeraiseprovide「10 percentage points' and '10% increase'workastwocorrectoption (one questiononlyhasonecorrectanswer). The wrong fossil values mustworkaserroroption. (13) Week 17 weekalready taught 3.1 (expressing one quantity as a percentage of another), Week 18 weekteach 3.2 (comparing two quantities by percentage), Week 19 weekteach 3.3 (percentages greater than 100%), This week we teach 3.4 (increasing/decreasing a quantity by a given percentage, including percentage point). This week don't teach 3.5 reverse percentages, 3.6 questions (aftercontinuecontent). Open the /learn page, Tell the child: 'This week's homework is here, after you finish, the system will auto-grade the multiple choice, write the equationpartneed Write out reasoning steps (show working steps), Simulate AEIS Part 2 gridformula. we'll sync progress in the We Chat group.'",
