 teacherNotes: "teachermodelfourcompleteexample questions (Write out reasoning steps). example questions 1 (Vertically opposite angles): Two lines cross at point O. One angle is 70°. Find the vertically opposite angle. Explain your answer. steps: ① Vertically opposite angles are equal (correcttopangleare equal). ② One angle is 70°. ③ The vertically opposite angle = 70°. Answer: 70° because vertically opposite angles are equal. example questions 2 (Angles on a straight line): A straight line has a ray from point O. One angle is 110°. Find the adjacent angle on the straight line. Explain your answer. steps: ① Angles on a straight line add to 180° (flatangleaboveangle and as 180°). ② One angle is 110°. ③ Adjacent angle = 180° − 110° = 70°. Answer: 70° because 110° + 70° = 180°. example questions 3 (Angles at a point): Three angles at point O are 120°, 150°, and x. Find x. steps: ① Angles at a point add to 360° (weekangleaboveangle and as 360°). ② 120° + 150° + x = 360°. ③ 270° + x = 360°. ④ x = 360° − 270° = 90°. Answer: x = 90°. example questions 4 (Explain fossil error): Wei says: 'Two lines cross. One angle is 70°. The vertically opposite angle is 110° because they add to 180°.' Explain what is wrong with Wei's statement. steps: ① Vertically opposite angles are equal, not supplementary (correcttopangleare equal, notmutuallysupplement). ② One angle is 70°. ③ The vertically opposite angle = 70° because they are equal. ④ Wei said 110° because he used the wrong property. Angles that add to 180° are angles on a straight line, not vertically opposite angles. Answer: Wei is wrong. The vertically opposite angle is 70°, not 110°. Vertically opposite angles are equal, so both angles are 70°. Angles on a straight line add to 180°, but these are vertically opposite angles, not angles on a straight line. teacherat whiteboard or screenabovewriteouteachquestionscompletesteps, anddrawoutmutualshouldfigure (two lines crossing, straight line with ray, angles at a point), childlookwithfollow. ",
