 teacherNotes: "On screen or on On the whiteboard, write the error example below: Wei says: 'A regular hexagon has 6 sides. So it has 5 lines of symmetry.' (mark as ✗). Ask the child: 「Is this correct? 'After the child thinks, circleerror: mixing up the number of sides and the number of lines of symmetry (error: confusesidenumber and correctaxis of symmetrynumberquantity). point out: Wei said a regular hexagon has 5 lines of symmetry, but this is wrong (Wei Saycorrectsixsideshapehas 5 itemcorrectaxis of symmetry, butThis iswrong). Why? Because in a regular polygon, the number of lines of symmetry EQUALS the number of sides (becausecorrectmanysideshapecorrectaxis of symmetrynumberquantityatsidenumber). A regular hexagon has 6 sides, so it has 6 lines of symmetry, not 5 (correctsixsideshapehas 6 itemside, sohas 6 itemcorrectaxis of symmetry, not 5). Write the corrections: Regular hexagon has 6 sides, so 6 lines of symmetry (mark as ✓). Have the child repeat the corrected sentences: Regular hexagon has 6 sides and 6 lines of symmetry. againgive Week two High-frequency errorexample: Aisha says: 'A regular pentagon has 5 sides. Each interior angle is 360° ÷ 5 = 72°.' (mark as ✗). Ask the child: 「Is this correct? 'circleerror: using 360° ÷ n as the interior angle (error: use 360° ÷ n whenworkinsideangle). point out: Aisha said each interior angle = 360° ÷ 5 = 72°, but this is wrong (Aisha Sayeachinsideangle = 360° ÷ 5 = 72°, butThis iswrong). Why? Because 360° ÷ n gives the EXTERIOR angle or the centre angle, not the interior angle (because 360° ÷ n calculateoutisoutsideangle or middlecenterangle, notinsideangle). The correct formula for the interior angle is (n − 2) × 180° ÷ n (correctinsideanglecommonformulais (n − 2) × 180° ÷ n). For a regular pentagon (n = 5): interior angle = (5 − 2) × 180° ÷ 5 = 3 × 180° ÷ 5 = 540° ÷ 5 = 108° (correctfivesideshape: insideangle = (5 − 2) × 180° ÷ 5 = 108°). The value 72° is the exterior angle or centre angle, not the interior angle (72° isoutsideangle or middlecenterangle, notinsideangle). Write the corrections: Each interior angle of a regular pentagon = (5 − 2) × 180° ÷ 5 = 108°, not 72°. 72° is the exterior or centre angle (mark as ✓). Have the child repeat the corrected sentences: Interior angle = 108°, not 72°. 72° is the exterior or centre angle.",
