 teacherNotes: "teachermodeltwocompleteexample questions (writewrite equation steps). example questions 1 (Parallelogram area): At Riverside Secondary, Mr Lim draws a parallelogram with base 8 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm. Question: Calculate the area of the parallelogram. Show your working. steps: ① Formula: Area of parallelogram = base × perpendicular height (commonformula: flatlinefoursideshapearea = base × perpendicularstraighthigh). ② Given: base = 8 cm, perpendicular height = 5 cm (alreadyknow: base = 8 cm, perpendicularstraighthigh = 5 cm). ③ Calculation: Area = 8 × 5 = 40 cm² (calculate: area = 8 × 5 = 40 cm²). ④ Answer: 40 cm² (answer: 40 cm²). at whiteboardwriteouteach step, sidewriteside Say. emphasize: Use the perpendicular height, not the slanted side. The slanted side is NOT the height (useperpendicularstraighthigh, notslantedside. slantedsidenothigh). example questions 2 (Trapezium area): At Riverside Secondary, Aisha studies a trapezium. The trapezium has parallel sides of length 6 cm and 10 cm, and perpendicular height 4 cm. Question: Calculate the area of the trapezium. Show your working. steps: ① Formula: Area of trapezium = ½ × (sum of the two parallel sides) × perpendicular height (commonformula: trapezoidarea = ½ × (twoflatlineside) × perpendicularstraighthigh). ② Given: parallel sides = 6 cm and 10 cm, perpendicular height = 4 cm (alreadyknow: flatlineside = 6 cm and 10 cm, perpendicularstraighthigh = 4 cm). ③ Calculation: Area = ½ × (6 + 10) × 4 = ½ × 16 × 4 = ½ × 64 = 32 cm² (calculate: area = ½ × (6 + 10) × 4 = ½ × 16 × 4 = 32 cm²). ④ Answer: 32 cm² (answer: 32 cm²). at whiteboardwriteouteach step, sidewriteside Say. emphasize: Do not forget the ½ in the trapezium formula. Common fossil: using (a + b)h instead of ½(a + b)h, which gives 64 cm² instead of the correct 32 cm² (notneedforgettrapezoidcommonformulamiddle ½. oftensee High-frequency error: use (a + b)h andnot ½(a + b)h, canto 64 cm² andnotcorrect 32 cm²). ",
