 duration: "2 min",
 teacherNotes: "Welcome child. 'Today is Week 81 week AEIS secondary math, we learn Sec 2 G2.7 perpendicular bisector of a line segment; angle bisector (perpendicularstraightflatdivideline and angleflatdivideline). This week only teach G2.7 only. Official content: (1) perpendicular bisector of a line segment AB: the line that passes through the midpoint of AB and is perpendicular to AB (perpendicularstraightflatdivideline: AB middlepointandperpendicularstraightat AB straight line). A point on the perpendicular bisector of AB is equidistant from A and B (perpendicularstraightflatdividelineabovepointto A and B distanceare equal). (2) angle bisector: the line that divides an angle into two equal parts (angleflatdivideline: oneangledividebecometwoare equalpartstraight line). A point on the angle bisector is equidistant from the two sides of the angle (angleflatdividelineabovepointtoangletwosidedistanceare equal). Simple locate/identify/find a length using those facts (simpledetermineplace/recognized't/usethissomepropertiesfindlongdegree). No compass-construction essay (noroundruleworkdiagramworklanguagequestions). Integer lengths only (or ½, ⅓ if the other length stays an integer) (only useintegerlongdegree, or person ½, ⅓ Ifanotheronesidekeepinteger), no calculator (without calculator). This week don't teach G2.6 scale drawings recap as the week's item (notworkasthis weekitemteachratiorulerdiagram Review); don't teach G2.8 determining congruent/similar triangles as a listed item (notworkascolumnitemteach Judgecongruent/similarthreeangle SSS/SAS/ASA/AAA); don't teach G2.9 ratio of areas = k² (don't teachareacompareat k²); don't teach G2.10 ratio of volumes = k³ (don't teachvolumecompareat k³); don't teach G4 Pythagoras/trigonometry (don't teach Pythagoreantheorem/threeanglefunctionnumber); don't teach G5 solids (don't teach3Dfigure). This week only teach G2.7: perpendicular bisector (point on it is equidistant from endpoints A and B) and angle bisector (point on it is equidistant from the two sides of the angle, and divides the angle into two equal parts).'",
