 teacherNotes: "summarythis weekfocus: (1) official Sec 2 AEIS math paper format: Part 1 MCQ 34 questions 30 min + Part 2 show working 20+10–15 questions 1 hour 45 min, without calculator. (2) this weekcontent: Sec 2 GEOMETRY AND MEASUREMENT, G2. Congruence and similarity: 2.7 perpendicular bisector of a line segment; angle bisector. This week is G2.7 only. Official content: (1) perpendicular bisector of a line segment AB: the line that passes through the midpoint of AB and is perpendicular to AB; a point on the perpendicular bisector of AB is equidistant from A and B (PA = PB). (2) angle bisector: the line that divides an angle into two equal parts (e.g. 80° → 40° + 40°); a point on the angle bisector is equidistant from the two sides of the angle (same perpendicular distance to both sides). Simple locate/identify/find a length using those facts. No compass-construction essay. Integer lengths only (or ½, ⅓ if other length stays integer), no calculator. (3) this week High-frequency error: mixing perpendicular bisector with angle bisector (perpendicular bisector: equidistant from endpoints A and B; angle bisector: equidistant from sides and divides angle into two equal parts; don't mix them); saying a point off the bisector is still equidistant (only points ON the bisector are equidistant; if the point is not on the bisector, it is not equidistant); two options that are the same pair in a different order (PA = 6 cm and PB = 6 cm is the same as PB = 6 cm and PA = 6 cm; only one option may be correct). Remember: perpendicular bisector → equidistant from endpoints. Angle bisector → divides angle into two equal parts AND point on it is equidistant from the two sides. Use these properties to find unknown lengths and angles. (4) this weekhomeworkat /learn/SMATH/81, include 5 reading, 8 grammar, 3 writing. Writing needwrite the equation and answer. completeafter AI cangiveoppositefeedback. hasaskquestionsfollowtimeconnectsystemteacher. belowweek, continue AEIS secondary math. ",
